1. Introduction
Plastics are widely used globally due to their low manufacturing costs, ease of use, versatility, durability, and stability (T ́abi et al., 2021). Annual production of plastics increased dramatically, from ~2 Mt. in 1950 to ~390 Mt. in 2021 (PEMRG, 2022). Plastics degrade into smaller particles through oxidation, hydrolysis, biodegradation, photodegradation, and mechanical processes (Sarkar et al., 2020). Microplastics (MPs), particles larger than 1 μm (Weber et al., 2022; >0.1 μm according to Koelmans et al., 2015) and smaller than 5 mm, can easily migrate by wind and water, making them extremely abundant in the atmosphere, soil, and water (Avio et al., 2017; Bullard et al., 2021), including in very remote areas (Ali, 2021). MPs are believed to pose a serious risk to living organisms including humans, animals, and plants (Leslie and Depledge, 2020; Zhang et al., 2022a; Djouina et al., 2023). Moreover, MPs can act as vectors to transport other contaminants adsorbed on their surface, such as heavy metals, pathogens (bacteria, fungi, protozoa, worms, viruses), and antibiotics (Gonza ́lez-Pleiter et al., 2021; Liu et al., 2022; Lu et al., 2022; Wang et al., 2021), posing additional risks to the environment and human health. Understanding MP transport in soil is important because of its broader implications for soil ecosystem and the environment. MPs have been shown to significantly affect soil structure (de Souza Machado et al., 2018), alter soil physiochemical properties (Medyn ́ska-Juraszek and Jadhav, 2022), disrupt nutrient cycling (Rong et al., 2021), influence biodiversity (Omidoyin and Jho, 2023) and the bioavailability of heavy metals (Feng et al., 2022), and affect the transport of other pollutants such as antibiotics chlortetracycline hydrochloride and tetracycline (Xu et al., 2022b; Zhao et al., 2020). These effects have long-term implications for soil health and productivity due to MPs can persist in soil for decades. MPs can be introduced into soils through various pathways. One prominent source is agricultural usage of plastic products such as plastic mulches, packaging materials, and plastic-based fertilizers (Fan et al., 2023). Other sources include leaching from waste landfills, direct (illegal) dumping of plastic materials, and irrigation and flooding of wastewaters, application of contaminated compost (Li et al., 2020b; Surendran et al., 2023). Additionally, the atmosphere contains airborne MPs from e.g. industrial activities and vehicle emissions, that can enter soils via precipitation and wind (Zhao et al., 2023). There is a growing interest in the fate of MPs in both soil and aquatic environments (Dioses-Salinas et al., 2020; Wong et al., 2020; Fig. S1 in the Supplementary Information (SI)). A significant challenge in understanding the fate of MPs in soils is that, unlike in oceans, MPs can strongly affect the host (soil) properties—specifically the properties that control their transport (de Souza Machado et al., 2018; Guo et al., 2022; Wang et al., 2022a), which in turn affects the MP fate. Recent reviews focused on the interaction of MPs with contaminants in soils (Chang et al., 2022; Yang et al., 2022); the sources, distribution, behavior, and detection techniques of MPs in soils (Li et al., 2020b; Koutnik et al., 2021; Yan and Yang, 2023); transport and transformation of MPs and nanoplastics (NPs) in soils (Liu et al., 2021); the migration of MPs in the soil-groundwater environment (Ren et al., 2021); the effects of MP on soil properties (Wang et al., 2022a); the occurrence, fate, transport, and ecological risk of MPs in soils (Zhou et al., 2020; Huerta Lwanga et al., 2022; Li et al., 2020b; Xu et al., 2020); and the deleterious
environmental effects of MPs in soils (Sajjad et al., 2022). Some reviews summarized the factors that affect MP transport in soil, e.g. including MP properties (e.g., size, shape, and type) and soil properties (e.g., porosity, texture, and composition) (Liu et al., 2021), as well as living organisms (Zhou et al., 2020). While these reviews shed light on how some of these properties affect MP transport, the impact of others remains elusive, with contradictory evidence of their effects that implies a more complex behavior of MPs than previously assumed. For example, Liu et al. (2021) indicated that smaller-size MPs have stronger mobility, in contrast to findings in subsequent studies (Qi et al., 2022; Gui et al., 2022). Recent research publications also pointed to further factors influencing MP transport, including hydrological conditions and living organisms (Gao et al., 2021; Xing et al., 2021; Gui et al., 2022; Li et al., 2021a; Luo et al., 2023b). MP transport in soils is expected to follow the tortuous migration pathways associated with the soil microstructure. While these pathways could have both a vertical and horizontal component, many of the studies of MPs transport and the metrics used to describe it refer to vertical transport, possibly because of the increasing difficulty of soil remediation as MPs gets deeper (e.g. if it contaminates groundwaters (Helmberger et al., 2020)). An added challenge is the inconsistency in the MP transport/mobility metrics and the way they are measured (Zhang et al., 2022b), making comparison between studies challenging. MP mobility metrics can be categorized into (a) flux- and (b) depthbased, measured by either mass or number of particles (Table 1). This paper provides a critical review of the state-of-the-art understanding of MP transport in soil. We point to several non-intuitive and often contradictory findings, demonstrating how these can be explained by the interference among factors and processes. Finally, we outline the knowledge gaps and propose future research directions.
2. Methodology and paper structure
To identify relevant papers, we conducted a literature search with the search term “microplastic” AND (“transport” OR “move” OR “mobile” OR “mobility”) AND (“soil” OR “porous media” OR “sand”) applied to title, abstract, keywords and full publication text. The search was conducted using the Google Scholar, PubMed, and Web of Science databases, and focused on papers published since 2000 (search last updated 22 July 2024; see Table S1 in SI for search details). Results were sorted according to relevance, and the searched literature was manually screened for papers that explicitly investigated the factors that affect microplastic transport in soil, excluding papers that coincidentally met the search criteria while being nonrelevant (e.g. papers that focused on NPs but mention “microplastics” in the text). Similarly, papers that focused on MP transport in aquatic environments (while mentioning briefly soils) or on the effect of MPs on transport of other substances were also excluded. Overall, we retained 113 papers that specifically addressed MP migration in soil, including 50 experimental studies. We note that our search results include papers that define particles in the 0.1 μm – 1.0 μm range as “microplastics” (as per the definition of Koelmans et al., 2015), while excluding papers that define such particles as “nanoplastics” (as per the definition of Weber et al., 2022). Many of the studies on MP transport in soil were conducted using artificially constructed soil analogs such as quartz sand or glass beads. These experiments, by simplifying visualization and/or analysis,
