n the SI list the experimental conditions classified in terms of the MP and soil properties, respectively.
3. Microplastic properties
This section focuses on the following MP properties: size, type, and shape, and their derivatives such as density, hydrophobicity, surface roughness, hydrodynamic diameter, and Zeta potential. We also discuss here MP aging, as a process altering other MP properties.
3.1. Microplastic particle size
A basic property that has a substantial influence on MP mobility in soil is MP size. Since MP size affects multiple mechanisms which control MP mobility, there is a complex trend: in general, MP mobility in soil for larger MPs (≳1 μm) is inversely proportional to the MP size, and it was found in terms of different MP mobility metrics (Table 1), including maximum depth (Ranjan et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2022b; O’Connor et al., 2019; Tumwet et al., 2022), proportion of MPs reaching the maximum depth (Cohen and Radian, 2022), mass recovery rate (Rong et al., 2022), and normalized concentration (Wang et al., 2022c). This is rather intuitive: for a given size of soil pores, smaller MPs can traverse further (Gao et al., 2021; Ranjan et al., 2023; O’Connor et al., 2019). In contrast, Qi et al. (2022) found that 20 μm MPs had higher mobility than 10 μm MPs, i.e. when water flow rate was 4 mL/ min, the MP concentrations of 10 and 20 μm MPs, went through soil, were 0.85 and 1.49 items/mL, respectively. The authors explained that
