 which the MP mobility trend reverses is not straightforward, as it can depend on other various properties. This can explain the apparent discrepancy between a critical size of 2 μm (for which MP normalized concentrations were higher than for 0.4 μm and 10 μm; Wang et al., 2022c) and 1 μm (for which MP mass recovery was greater than for 0.2 μm and 2 μm; Rong et al., 2022).
3.2. Microplastic shape
Microplastics are found in natural soils in a variety of shapes (Fig. S2 in SI), with the most common being fragments (irregular-shaped), fibers, films, foams, and beads (spheres) (Sajjad et al., 2022). While it is accepted that MP shapes affect their transport, the link between MP shapes and their transport in soils remains elusive, also due to the large number of additional parameters which vary among experiments probing the impact of MP shapes (Tables S2-S3 in SI). Beads were found to reach deeper than films because films were trapped more easily by plant roots (Li et al., 2021a). Fragments were found to move deeper than fibers (Gao et al., 2021; Cohen and Radian, 2022; Waldschla ̈ger and Schüttrumpf, 2020), possibly due to the tendency of fibers to become entangled with soil grains (de Souza Machado et al., 2018; Waldschl ̈ager and Schüttrumpf, 2020; Keller et al., 2019). Similarly, spheres reach a deeper layer than fragments (irregular-shaped) because fragments can easily be entangled with soil particles (Waldschla ̈ger and Schüttrumpf, 2020). Zhang et al. (2022b), fibers reached deeper than fragments, which in turn were more mobile than foams and films. However, it is hard to draw conclusive evidence regarding MP fiber mobility from Zhang et al. (2022b), as the authors did not specify the fiber diameter, which has a greater effect on MP infiltration than its length (Waldschl ̈ager and Schüttrumpf, 2020). The above suggests that fiber, fragments, and beads tend to migrate deeper than films and foams.
3.3. Microplastics type
Different MP types (made from different polymers) have different properties including density, hydrophobicity (contact angle), surface roughness, and Zeta potential (O’Connor et al., 2019; Fei et al., 2022;
Ranjan et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2021; Cohen and Radian, 2022). Each of these properties can affect various aspects of MP transport. In general, heavier MPs have greater vertical mobility. For example, polyethylene
(PE; density of 0.884–0.898 g/cm3) reached deeper than polypropylene
(PP; density of 0.833 g/cm3) (O’Connor et al., 2019). However, surface properties often dominate over density: lower hydrophobicity, higher Zeta potential, and lower roughness were found to have a stronger positive effect on MP transport in soil than higher density (Fei et al., 2022; Ranjan et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2021; Cohen and Radian, 2022). Despite its lower density, the mass recovery rate of polylactic acid (PLA;
density of 1.25 g/cm3) was higher than polyvinyl chloride (PVC; density
of 1.38 g/cm3), because PLA has higher Zeta potential and lower hydrophobicity (Fei et al., 2022). Comparing PE, polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and PP, the order of PE > PET>PP was identified in terms of depth reached (Ranjan et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2021), again explained by the lower hydrophobicity and higher Zeta potential of PE vs. PET vs. PP, despite its lower density (all other conditions were uniform). The lower mobility of PP was explained by its low density and Zeta potential, and its higher hydrophobicity and surface roughness (Ranjan et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2021). Lower hydrophobicity was used to explain deeper penetration of polyamide (PA) (vs. PE, PET, and PP in Gao et al., 2021; and vs. PET in terms of depth ratio in Cohen and Radian, 2022). Inconsistencies between studies, e.g. deeper penetration of PET vs. PE in Gao et al. (2021), opposite to the trend found by Zhang et al. (2022b), could be due to the different mobility metrics: PET exhibited larger penetration depth than PE, however its relative mobility was lower (Zhang et al. (2022b)). Additional potential sources of inconsistency are due to using different analytical methods for sample preparation and analysis of MPs in the MP transport experiments (Table S5). Differences in other experimental parameters, including the soil properties, hydraulic conditions, and MP size and shape might also cause inconsistency between studies.
3.4. Microplastic concentration
The concentration of MPs does not always correlate with their mobility. Xu et al. (2022a, 2022b) showed that higher concentration of MPs (for two sizes, 0.1 μm and 2 μm) resulted in a higher mass recovery rate. This is due to the adsorption of some MPs on soil particles which then depleted the available sites for adsorption with increasing MP concentration and, therefore, enhancing MP mobility. Contrasting findings in smaller particles (≤ 0.1 μm), where the mobility (total recovery rate) showed inverse correlation with their concentration (Wang et al., 2022e). Contradictory findings for plastic particles ≤0.1 μm with increasing their concentration are probably due to the increasing dominance of mechanisms as plastic particle size decreases, including: (i) aggregation and deposition (Wang et al., 2022e); (ii) adsorption on soil particles due to the increase in plastic particle specific surface area (Shen et al., 2019); as well as (iii) clogging of pores by plastic particles. This contraction may also be due to differences in other MPs and soil properties between these studies (Tables S2-S3 in SI). Flow rates and soil particle size can also interplay with MP concentrations to affect MP transport. For example, in 1–2 mm sand, while at high-enough water flow rates (2.5 mL/min in Hou et al., 2020) MP mobility shows the expected proportionality with MP concentration, at lower flow rates (1–2 mL/min) no such correlation was found (Hou et al., 2020). A possible explanation is that MPs can be more easily intercepted by sand particles at slow flow rates, with faster flow rates allowing easier flushing of MPs reducing such interceptions (Hou et al., 2020). Additionally, at a water flow rate of 1–1.5 mL/min, the MP normalized concentration increased with increase of MP concentration in 2–4 mm sand, while at 1–2 mm sand no such correlation was found. A possible explanation is that the blockage of MP particles in finer soils is more apparent (Hou et al., 2020).
