3.5. Microplastic aging
Aging of MPs, which can occur by oxidation, hydrolysis, biodegradation, photodegradation, and mechanical processes, can alter a variety of physical and chemical properties of MPs. The mobility of aged MPs was found to be greater than that of pristine MPs, in terms of both MP concentration (Qi et al., 2022) and maximum depth (Wang et al., 2022b). One reason is that aging increases MP oxygen-containing groups (Ali et al., 2023), which can reduce MP adsorption on soil particles (Qi et al., 2022; Yan et al., 2020). Furthermore, aging alters polymer structure, reducing MP size and changing their shape (Ren et al., 2021; Yan et al., 2020). For example, Mao et al. (2020) found that the C–H bond of microplastics breaks under the action of UV light, causing MP to break into smaller particles (Mao et al., 2020). Breaking into smaller MPs, which is known to enhance MP mobility (Ranjan et al., 2023), as well as reduce MP hydrodynamic diameter (Wang et al., 2022b). Aging also increases MP Zeta potential, and hence the interaction energy between MPs and soil particles (Wang et al., 2022b), and can increase MP surface hydrophilicity, promoting MP transport in soil (Gunther et al., 2023; Li et al., 2021a). Notably, aging can increase the surface roughness of MP (Luo et al., 2023a), which may lead to interlocking between the rough surfaces of MPs and the irregularly shaped soil particles, thereby decreasing MP mobility. Further research is needed to evaluate the relative impact of this mechanism vs. that of other, competing effects of MP aging (which facilitate MP migration) including reduced size, increased surface functionality, and enhanced hydrophilicity.
4. Soil physicochemical properties
This section reviews the impact of soil physical and chemical properties including soil texture, grain/pore size, soil pH, ionic strength (IS), soil mineralogy, and organic components, on MP transport. We also include here the impact of chemical substances present in the soil, as, while they are not soil properties per se, they do affect soil chemistry.
4.1. Soil texture, grain and pore size
Overall, coarser soil texture promotes MP transport; this was found in terms of mass recovery rate (Dong et al., 2022; Wu et al., 2020), maximum depth (Mancini et al., 2023), transport rate (Gui et al., 2022), MP concentration (number of MPs per kg soil) at maximum depth (Xing et al., 2021), and loss ratio (Rehm et al., 2021). For a given MP size, larger soil particles, which in turn imply larger pore sizes, enhance MP mobility in soil, in terms of mass recovery rate (Dong et al., 2022; Hou et al., 2020), maximum depth (Gao et al., 2021; Ranjan et al., 2023), and MP concentration (Qi et al., 2022). The control parameter when varying both MP size and soil texture can be evaluated using the ratio of the diameter of MP particles (DMP) vs. that of the soil particles (DS), where a smaller ratio promotes MP transport in soil (Waldschl ̈ager and Schüttrumpf, 2020). The critical ratio can be evaluated theoretically by considering the size of a constriction in the simple case of a hexagonal packing of equal-sized spheres: the narrowest aperture (between each triplet of particles) is ~0.1DS, indicating a critical ratio of ~0.1. Experimental data agrees with this estimation: MPs were found to be highly mobile for DMP/DS < 0.1, and nearly immobile for DMP/DS > 0.3 (Gao et al., 2021; Ranjan et al., 2023). Similarly, the infiltration depth of MPs was found to reduce from 13.1 cm to 4.63 cm, and 1.18 cm at DMP/ DS < 0.11, 0.11 < DMP/DS < 0.32, and DMP/DS > 0.32, respectively. However, we note that because most soils are composed of a wide range of particle sizes, the average grain diameter DS is insufficient to describe the impact of soil texture on MP mobility, which may require to consider a more comprehensive parameter such as particle size distribution. In addition to the obvious effect of larger soil pores in coarse-textured soils (Dong et al., 2022; Xing et al., 2021), larger soil particles have lower specific surface area, and therefore, adsorb and retain MPs less than finer soils. Soil texture also affects soil Zeta potential. In most cases, both soil
and MP surfaces are negatively charged; in such cases, coarser soils have higher Zeta potential (Dong et al., 2022; Rong et al., 2022; Gui et al., 2022; Li et al., 2023), and thus stronger repulsion promoting MP mobility (in terms of mass recovery rate in Wu et al., 2020). MP surface roughness was found to reduce MP penetration depth, due to increased friction with the soil pores (Ranjan et al., 2023). However, this effect is subject to interference with other mechanisms and properties, including water flow rate and MP concentration (Hou et al., 2020). Higher porosity was also found to promote MP mass recovery rate (Li et al., 2023; Dong et al., 2022). We stress however that porosity, being a scalar which represents a sample-averaged property, is a too simplistic soil characteristic to provide a clear trend of MP mobility. For instance, soil can have high porosity which is poorly connected (e.g. with dead-end pores), such that it has limited ability to transport water and MPs. This could potentially explain the seemingly-contradictory result of higher MP mass recovery rate in low-porosity desert soil than in highporosity red soil (Wu et al., 2020).
4.2. Mineralogy of soil grains
Soil mineralogy affects MP transport in two ways. Firstly, forming MP-minerals aggregates, which have a higher density than MPs alone, can enhance the depth of MPs penetration (Yan et al., 2020). Secondly, MP-minerals aggregates can decrease MP mobility in soil. For instance, Li et al. (2019), Li et al. (2020a), and Gui et al. (2022) found that the forming of MP-Fe/Al oxide aggregates decreased the MP mass recovery rate and transport rate because the diameter of the MP-Fe/Al oxide aggregates was larger than individual MP particles. The effect of MPminerals aggregates on MP transport might be MP size dependent (< 5 mm in Yan et al., 2020, vs. 0.02–2 μm in Li et al., 2019; Li et al., 2020a, vs. 0.3–1 μm in Gui et al., 2022). Koutnik et al. (2022a) found that the MP density plays a greater role for the movement of larger MPs (> 50 μm); however, the intermolecular force plays a greater role than MP density for the movement of small MPs (<50 μm). Soil minerals affect MP transport by affecting the MP Zeta potential and the interaction of MPs and minerals (Yan et al., 2020; Li et al., 2019; Li et al., 2020a; Li et al., 2021b; Wu et al., 2020; Gui et al., 2022). Yan et al. (2020) found that natural soil minerals (soil minerals were not specified by authors) enhanced the penetration depth of MPs because they make the MP Zeta potential more negative. In contrast, Wu et al. (2020) and Gui et al. (2022) found that the presence of Fe or Al oxide minerals (Fe/Al oxides) decreased MP mobility (both mass recovery rate and transport rate) because MPs were adsorbed on the surface of Fe/Al oxides. Fe/Al oxides were also found to limit MP transport by reducing the volume of larger pores (Lu et al., 2014). Thus, highly weathered soils such as Ultisols, which typically contain significant quantities of Fe and Al oxides, may act as barriers to MP movement. The contrasting findings of Yan et al. (2020) versus Wu et al. (2020) and Gui et al. (2022) might be due to difference in the electrical properties of different minerals, as well as the different mobility metrics (rate- vs. depth-based). The effect of Fe and Al oxides on MP transport is pH-dependent because their surface charge is altered depends on pH: it is positive for acidic and neutral pH, and it becomes negative above pH 8 (for example in hematite, due to the deprotonation of Fe-OH; Nie et al., 2023). Kaolinite and bentonite particles were found to increase the mass recovery rate of MPs at pH 6 and IS 5 mM NaCl because they can act as a carrier for MPs, thus promoting MP mobility (Li et al., 2020a; Li et al., 2021b). However, bentonite would decrease the MP mobility at IS of 25 mM NaCl because the diameter of MP-bentonite aggregates was around 3 times larger than individual MP particle (Li et al., 2021b).
4.3. Organic components in soils
