4.4. pH
Both MP mass recovery rate and normalized concentration were found to increase with increasing pH (Fei et al., 2022; Wu et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2022e). This is due to the Zeta potential of both MPs and soil minerals becoming more negative with increasing pH (Fig. 3b~d), indicating a stronger repulsive force between them (Fig. 2a~b). The repulsion (associated with electrostatic forces) was suggested as the dominant factor promoting MP mass recovery rate with increasing pH (Wu et al., 2020). Fei et al. (2022) and Wang et al. (2022e) found that the MP hydrodynamic diameter increases with decreasing pH and, therefore, decreasing MP mass recovery. Nie et al. (2023) found that increasing pH from 3 to 8.5 reversed the interaction force of MPs and minerals (from attraction to repulsion), decreasing MP-minerals aggregation (Nie et al., 2023). Overall, the repulsive force between MP and sand as well as other soil minerals increases with increasing pH (Fig. 2b).
4.5. Ionic strength
In general, MP mobility decreases with increasing IS (Chu et al., 2019; Dong et al., 2022; Hou et al., 2020; Wu et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2022e; Li et al., 2021b; found in terms of both MP mass recovery rate, and MP normalized concentration). The Zeta potential of both soil particles and MPs increases with increasing IS (Wang et al., 2022e; Hou et al., 2020; Cao et al., 2023). The repulsive force between sand and microplastic increased with decreasing IS (Chu et al., 2019). Moreover, the hydrodynamic diameter of MPs increases with increasing IS, which leads to enhanced retention and reduced transport of MPs (Cao et al., 2023). Different types of MPs can show relatively different mobility at different IS due to specific characteristics (e.g., chemical composition; presence of functional groups on the surface of MPs). For example, Cao et al. (2023) found that the total mass recovery of PVC and PP MPs was relatively lower than PMMA and PET MPs for increasing IS. It was found
that Ca2+ has a greater effect on MP retention than K+, Na+, and Mg2+
(Dong et al., 2021), and that Al3+ has a greater effect on MP retention
than Na+ and Mg2+ (Fei et al., 2022) because cations with higher ionic radii lead to stronger charge screening, which increases particle retention (Dong et al., 2021; Wu et al., 2020). In soil contaminated by MPs, different IS have different effects on the MP migration because the degree of aggregation of soil mineral particles with MPs can be different under different IS. For example, ~77 % and ~ 97 % of bentonites were adsorbed onto MPs forming MP-bentonite hetero-aggregates when IS was 5 mM and 25 mM NaCl, respectively; the diameter of the MPbentonite hetero-aggregates increased with increasing IS, which in turn decreased the MP mass recovery rate (Li et al., 2021b).
4.6. Chemical substances in soils
Chemical substances that might be present in soil, such as nitrogen fertilizers, antibiotics, nonylphenol, emerging pollutants (e.g., perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA), as part of per- and polyfluorinated substances (PFAS)), and biochar, can also impact MP transport. Nitrogen fertilizers were found to decrease the MP mass recovery rate because they decreased the Zeta potential of both MPs and soil particles and increased the hydrodynamic diameter of MPs (Fig. 2a, c; Rong et al.,
2022). Chlortetracycline hydrochloride (a widely used antibiotic in agriculture) was shown to decrease the MP mass recovery rate, due to a decline in the electrostatic repulsion between MP and sand grains (Xu et al., 2022b). The large specific surface area of biochar was shown to provide more adsorption sites for MPs, hindering their transport (Wang et al., 2022d), with the stronger impact of Fe3O4 biochar because its surface roughness, specific surface area, and the total pore volume are much higher than normal biochar, indicating that the Fe3O4 biochar is more beneficial to the MP retention (Wang et al., 2022f; Tong et al., 2020). Unlike biochar and nitrogen fertilizers, nonylphenol (a non-ionic surfactant present in pesticide formulation) increases the MP mass recovery rate, as it increases the MP Zeta potential, and possibly because of the hydrogen bonds forming between MPs and nonylphenol (Xu et al., 2022a). The presence of surfactants in soil alters the surface hydrophobicity of microplastic, influencing MP transport (Sun et al., 2022; Jiang et al., 2021). Both cationic surfactants and anionic surfactants were found to promote the MP mass recovery rate, as long as the surfactant concentration remained lower than the critical micelle concentration; this was explained by the increase in the Zeta potential of MPs and sand particles and the decrease of MP hydrophobicity by the surfactants (Jiang et al., 2021). Cationic surfactants were found to have a greater effect on MP transport than anionic surfactants because the former provides more osmotic and elastic repulsion forces between MPs and sand particles (Jiang et al., 2022). Surfactants have a nontrivial effect with MP types (PE and PP) on MP mobility: while the mobility of PE was higher than of PP without surfactants (in terms of maximum depth; Gao et al., 2021; O’Connor et al., 2019; Ranjan et al., 2023), the addition of surfactants reversed that trend (in terms of MP mass recovery rate; Jiang et al., 2021, 2022; Zhou et al., 2024). The authors explained this by the increase in Zeta potential, as well as the larger surface area and roughness of PP MPs, promoting surfactant retention (Jiang et al., 2021). Zhou et al. (2024) attribute this to the PP hydrophilicity being higher than PE hydrophilicity when adding surfactants. However, we note that the use of two different MP mobility metrics across these studies does not allow a decisive conclusion regarding the main reason for the reversal in mobility of PP and PE. Zhao et al. (2020) found that the effect of tetracycline (TC) on the of MP mobility depends on the type of solution and IS. For example, in KCl or CaCl2 solutions at IS 1 mM, TC increased the mass recovery of MP because TC decreased MP hydrophobicity, promoting MP mobile. However, at IS 100 mM, TC decreased the MP mobility in the KCl solution but increased the MP mobility in the CaCl2 solution. The authors attributed this to the presence of TC rendered the attachment efficiencies of MP less sensitive to the changes in the CaCl2 solutions (Zhao et al., 2020). Similarly, titanium dioxide nanoparticles (nTiO2) decreased the mass recovery of MP because they formed aggregates with MPs, which increased the diameter of MP compared to individual MP (Cai et al., 2019). PFOA (a widely used industrial surfactant) was found to decrease the normalized concentration of negatively-charged MPs, thereby decreasing their Zeta potential, and promoting their adsorption on soil particles; an opposite effect—increasing normalized concentration—was observed for positively-charged MPs (carboxyl group modified MPs), due to the reversal of their surface charge, limiting their adsorption on soil particles (Rong et al., 2023). In contrast, Xu et al. (2024a) found that PFOA increases the recovery rate of MPs because PFOA induces a more negatively charged MP surface.
5. Hydrological conditions
Since transport of MPs in soils is driven by water infiltration, the hydrological conditions that control how water is introduced into the soil play a decisive role. The interaction of MPs with water is complex: in addition to the amount of water and its velocity, the transport of small MPs also depends on the motion of air-water interfaces in the vadose zone. The fact that MPs are hydrophobic further complicates their
