migration, as explained below.
5.1. Water flux
Higher water flux in general promotes MP transport (Fei et al., 2022; Qi et al., 2022; Wang et al., 2022c; Dong et al., 2022). This is mainly due to the increase in the shear force which sweeps MPs with the water flow; however, slower water infiltration allows more transverse (horizontal) flow, which could enhance MP transport by allowing the water to traverse larger MPs-contaminated areas in the soil (shown in terms of mass recovery rate in Hou et al. (2020) and Fei et al. (2022)). Higher water velocity was shown to increase the detachment of MPs from the soil matrix (Wang et al., 2022c; Qi et al., 2022; Dong et al., 2022). Further intricacies can arise due to the interactions of flow rates with factors including IS, MP concentration, and pore size (Dong et al., 2022; Hou et al., 2020). Dong et al. (2022) showed a positive correlation between water velocity and MP mass recovery rate under high IS (10 Mm NaCl), albeit with negligible influence of velocity on MP transport at lower IS (0.1 Mm NaCl). Another nontrivial relationship between MP transport and water flux is linked to MP concentration and soil particle size, showing an initial increase in MP mass recovery rate with velocity followed by a decrease at even higher velocities, for MP concentrations of 0.3 mg/L and 0.5 mg/ L in 1–2 and 2–4 mm sand, respectively (Hou et al., 2020). The authors attributed this decline in MP mobility to an increase in MP adsorption to soil particles due to increase in frequency of collisions between MP particles with flow rate, occupying a larger portion of the pore space. All these factors increase the probability of MPs to adsorb on the soil particles. We note most studies on the effect of flow rate were conducted in coarser soils, where flow rates would be higher than in finer soils.
5.2. Soil water saturation
Soil water saturation (the relative pore volume occupied by water, changing by wetting/drying) also affects MP transport (Dong et al., 2022). This might be due to the hydrophobic nature of MPs, minimized contact of MPs with water, and MPs can remain trapped within the airwater interface (Al Harraq and Bharti, 2022). As decrease in water saturation produces more air-water interfaces, this in turn limits MP migration in soils (Dong et al., 2022). Another reason for this trend is the increase in trapped air bubbles, that decrease the available pore space for migration of water and MPs (Dong et al., 2022). For low density MPs, however, increasing water saturation was shown to decrease MP mass recovery rate because of buoyancy effects (O’Connor et al., 2019; Li et al., 2021a).
5.3. Wetting-drying cycles
Since MP transport is strongly linked to water saturation (Dong et al., 2022) and to the air-water interfaces (Al Harraq and Bharti, 2022), cycles of wetting and drying (which cause the interfaces to advance and recede repeatedly) play a crucial role. A positive correlation between the number of cycles and the maximum MP depth was reported for various settings (Gao et al., 2021; O’Connor et al., 2019; Ranjan et al., 2023; Zhao et al., 2022b). In addition to interface motion, wetting-drying cycles are associated with soil surface damage, in particular, cracks formed by repeated shrinkage and expansion (Tang et al., 2021; Wan et al., 2019), serving as preferential pathways for MPs. Another mechanism associated with wetting-drying cycles which affects MP transport is mechanical abrasion and weathering of MPs resulting in fragmentation into smaller particles; this was used to explain increased MPs penetration depth with wetting-drying cycles (Ranjan et al., 2023).
5.4. Freeze-thaw cycles
Freeze-thaw cycles can promote MP mobility, by (1) releasing
colloids from the soil to which MPs can attach and, therefore, increasing MP mobility; and (2) applying cyclic mechanical pressure on pore walls, creating additional preferential flow pathways (Li et al., 2023; Koutnik et al., 2022b). Contrasting evidence showed that these cycles caused aggregation of MPs, decreasing their mobility (in terms of MP normalized concentration in Alimi et al., 2021). The difference of plastic size was suggested as explanation for these contrasting results (Alimi et al., 2021). The impact of freeze-thaw cycles depends on the type of soil: it was found to increase mobility in loamy sand and sandy soil, with no apparent effect in quartz sand media (in terms of MP normalized concentration) (Li et al., 2023). The authors explained that the loamy sand and sandy soils released more colloids than quartz sand media. However, Koutnik et al. (2022b) found that although a sand and soil mixture (biofilter media) could release more colloids, the freeze-throw cycles increased greater MP mobility (normalized concentration) in sand than that in biofilter media because the pore sizes in sand were larger.
6. Living organisms
Living organisms such as soil animals, plants, and microorganisms, affect MP transport (Huerta Lwanga et al., 2017, 2018; Rillig et al., 2017; Li et al., 2021a; He et al., 2020, 2021), with a larger effect exhibited by larger living species (Ren et al., 2021). It has been shown that soil animals (e.g. earthworms) can promote MP migration by (1) creating macro-pores that serve as preferential MP pathways; and (2) digesting MPs, transporting them further to where MPs are either excreted as defecate or released once the organism dies (Huerta Lwanga et al., 2017; Rillig et al., 2017). Plant roots enhance the penetration depth of MP because their decomposition leaves a macropore for MP transport. MPs in contact with the root tips of plants tend to migrate with the growth of the root system; thus, MP transport will depend on the root orientation, i.e. horizontal vs. vertical (Li et al., 2021a). Rhizosphere secretion can also induce MPs aging and decrease their hydrophobicity, increasing MP mobility (Li et al., 2021a). Microscopic organisms, i.e. bacteria, affect MP transport by altering both the soil and the MP properties (He et al., 2020, 2021). For example, He et al. (2020) found that the gram-negative strain E. coli was shown to decrease the MP mass recovery rate by creating a biofilm which narrows the soil pores as well as increases their surface roughness, as well as by decreasing the repulsive force between MPs and soil particles. Bacteria interact with MPs in a complex manner, by forming MPs-bacteria aggregates (He et al., 2021). Bacteria reduce the Zeta potential of negatively charged MPs (the Zeta potential of MPs-bacteria aggregates is lower than that of only MPs) and increase the hydrodynamic diameter (larger for aggregates than for individual MPs); both effects were shown to decrease the MP mass recovery rate. For positively charged MPs, bacteria will reverse the MP charge (as the overall charge of the aggregates is negative), increasing the MP mass recovery rate in soils with negatively charged grains. In addition, IS influences the effect trend of types of bacteria on MP transport. For example, the reductions of the mass recovery of negatively charged MP by E. coli are less than by B. subtilis (in both IS = 5 mM and IS = 25 mM). The increase of the mass recovery of positively-charged MP by B. subtilis is higher than E. coli when the IS was 5 mM; however, this phenomenon was reversed when the IS was 25 mM where E. coli increased the zeta potential of MP more negatively than that of B. subtilis, likely influencing MP mass recovery (He et al., 2021).
7. Discussion
Microplastic transport is affected by a multitude of physical, chemical, and biological parameters, many of which are intimately coupled. These include properties of: (i) the MPs—size, shape, concentration and type (density, surface roughness and wettability), and the MP zeta potential; (ii) the soil—texture, pore and particle sizes, mineralogy, the zeta potential of soil particle and soil organic matter; (iii) hydrological
