conditions—flow rate, wetting-drying cycles, saturation, and soil solution geochemistry including pH, ionic strength, and chemicals present in soil; and (iv) soil living organisms—plants, animals and microorganisms. The complex dependence of MP mobility on various processes and properties can be illustrated by relating MP mobility to basic MP properties (type, size, and shape) and soil texture. When all basic parameters are plotted together no clear trend emerges, although some trends can be identified within subsets of the data, i.e. for specific properties (Fig. 4). Given these properties are categorical variables (i.e. with no inherent standalone numerical context), we categorized the data set into 17 unique subsets based on various MP characteristics (Table S7 in SI). However, due to limited amount of data in many of these series, we focus our analysis on a few subsets only. Considering flux-based mobility proxies as the dependent variable, we find for two subsets—(i) PE fragment in fine soil and (ii) PS beads in fine soil—a statistically significant negative correlation between MP size and flux-based mobility (p-values of 0.062 and 0.032, significant at the 10 % and 5 % levels, respectively). In contrast, for PS beads in very fine soil, MP size exhibit a statistically significant positive correlation with flux-based mobility (pvalue = 0.046, significant at the 5 % level). For depth-based MP mobility, PE fragments in coarse soil exhibit a statistically significant negative correlation between MP size and depth (p-value = 0.018, significant at the 5 % level, see Figs. S3–5 in SI). Beyond these, no significant correlations were observed for other parameters, suggesting competing effects among these properties. Hence, the effect of some parameters on MP mobility depends on interactions with other parameters. A formidable source of complexity which challenges our understanding of MP mobility is the interaction of the aforementioned factors to affect mobility, termed here “co-effects”. By co-effects we refer here to the interference among factors, where the simultaneous changes in two or more parameters alter the competition between various factors and processes. These co-effects are behind the complex and often contradictory findings presented in this review, and the lack of clear trend in MP mobility when plotted vs. a single factor, e.g. the dependence of MP transport on MP size in Fig. 4. While the co-effect of MP sizes and soil pore size is intuitive—larger pore sizes allow larger MPs to pass through, other co-effects of MP size may be less clear. For instance, as MP size
decreases below a certain threshold (which in turn could depend on multiple factors such as soil solution chemistry), additional mechanisms which affect transport become more dominant. For MP sizes in the range of 0.1–1 μm, a decrease in MP size decreases their Zeta potential, which enhances MP adsorption on the soil particles, reducing their mobility (reversing the trend for MPs larger than ~1 μm). Aggregation of smaller plastic particles (< 0.1 μm, i.e. NPs) with ionic compounds from the soil solution (a process which by itself is also moderated by the plastics’ zeta potential), increases the aggregates’ hydrodynamic diameter; this, in turn, can reverse again the mobility trend to be inversely proportional (similar to the inverse proportionality between MP size and mobility in larger MPs, i.e. > 1 μm). We note that only co-effects evidenced in the literature are presented
