in Fig. 5; we expect more co-effects to exist, for example, between the Zeta potentials of the MP and the soil, to be revealed upon further examination. Exploring the interactions among multiple factors and the resulting co-effects (Fig. 5) requires further experimental studies where all parameters besides those investigated are controlled and known. In many cases, multiple parameters that are not the control parameters are unknown or vary together with other parameters, challenging conclusive evidence on the specific effect of the actual control parameter of interest. Another challenge in our understanding of MP mobility in soils is the lack of standard metrics and standard methods of analysis quantifying it. Currently, published studies vary in the application of sampling methods (e.g. collecting effluent samples from the column transport experiments, extracting MPs from soil or sediment samples), in sample preparation methods for MP analysis (e.g. sonication, magnetic stirring, wet sieving, density separation using different chemicals, digestion of organic matter), and in methods for identification and quantification of MPs (e.g. Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy, UV–Vis spectrophotometry, fluorescence spectrophotometry, stereomicroscopy, scanning and transmission electron microscopy) (Table S5). Similarly, there are multiple metrics which are defined differently for MP mobility (Table 1). While some of these metrics are correlated (meaning that higher values of these metrics indicate greater MP mobility), they do not represent identical phenomena nor carry the same environmental implications, and thus cannot be used interchangeably. For example, a scenario where only a few MPs reach very deep would be characterized by a low transport rate and loss ratio, however a large maximum penetration depth (Zhang et al., 2022b). These differences in methods of analysis and in metrics not only hinder the cross-comparison of published studies and the development of a unified understanding of MP behavior in soils, but also limit the applicability of findings to broader environmental contexts. Addressing this challenge thus requires the development and adoption of standardized protocols and metrics that enable consistent and reproducible evaluations of MP transport in soil.
8. Conclusion and future perspectives
In conclusion, our ability to predict MP transport in soils is hindered by several knowledge gaps, owing to the complex underlying physics, chemistry, and biology. This scientific challenge makes MP transport a vibrant, exciting topic that is becoming intensely explored by various disciplines. We point to the need for further fundamental understanding of the transport mechanisms controlling how hydrophobic MP particles move in heterogenous porous materials; some of this knowledge can only be addressed by first studying idealized systems, followed by more applied studies which incorporate the complexity of natural soils and the various environmental conditions. Specifically, we flag the following research needs:
(i) Gaining fundamental insights into the physics of the MP transport process, in particular for very small MPs where colloidal forces may become important, through the use of soil analogs such as glass beads and micromodels; these insights should be then tested against experiments using natural soils, in particular clay-rich, where the complex geochemistry and pore size distribution may govern MP transport; (ii) Extending the range of MP sizes for different MP types; currently all studies on very small MPs (< 20 μm) are performed with polystyrene particles only; (iii) Understanding the transport of MPs when other particles adsorb on them, and the potential impact of changes in the surface properties of the aggregate (vs. the MP alone); this is also highlyrelevant for transport of heavy metals, pathogens, and antibiotics by MPs acting as vectors;
(iv) Examining the role of preferential pathways, which prevail in hydrophobic soils, and the role played by the hydrophobic MPs—known to induce such pathways, on MP transport; (v) Exposing the impact of the temporal evolution of MP properties as they age and degrade with changing environmental conditions; (vi) Identifying additional interactions among multiple factors and co-effects.
We note that some of the controlling factors are less studied than others, probably due to technical challenges rather than their lesser importance; for example, the role of MP shape and of MP composites (as many plastics are not made of a single polymer). Another, more technical challenge is the use of different MP mobility metrics, e.g. maximum depth reached within a sample vs. concentration traversing the entire sample, which refer to disparate aspects of MP mobility that cannot be compared quantitatively, and of different environmental implications. The development of standardized analytical methods for the identification and quantification of microplastics in soil and their comparison in benchmark studies are essential to facilitate meaningful comparisons, identify consistent trends, and enhance the reliability and impact of quantitative reviews across research studies. The broad, interdisciplinary community studying MP transport would benefit from the research incorporating the above aspects in a systematic and synergetic manner. These scientific advancements can also improve our ability to monitor and mitigate MP pollution, as well as develop less-polluting materials, and improve policies for waste management, treatment, and mitigation.
